    description: "Tracker `add`/`update` accept descriptions only via the `--description \"...\"` argument, which means the content has to survive shell quoting. Inside double quotes, bash still performs **command substitution** on backticks (`` ` ``), variable expansion on `$VAR`, and arithmetic expansion on `$((...))`. Markdown code spans use backticks; tech-writing descriptions routinely include `function_name()`, file paths in backticks, and command examples — every one of those is one keystroke away from silent corruption.\n\n**Concrete failure mode** (observed 2026-04-07 while creating WI-kugob):\n\n```\nscripts/tracker add ... --description \"...Run `auto-pr prune` to remove stale entries...\"\n```\n\nBash interpreted the backticks, tried to execute `auto-pr prune`, got `auto-pr: command not found`, and substituted an empty string. The tracker CLI received `\"...Run  to remove stale entries...\"` (note the two spaces) and stored it without complaint. The only signal was a `command not found` line in stderr that's easy to miss in a long tool output. Caught by chance during a verification read; would otherwise have shipped a corrupted description.\n\n**Why escaping is not a sufficient fix:** an agent (or human) writing descriptions has no good defense. Backslash-escaping every backtick (`` \\` ``) works but is brittle and easy to forget. Switching to single quotes works but breaks the moment the description contains an apostrophe — and tracker descriptions routinely include English contractions and possessives. Heredocs work but require multi-line bash gymnastics for what should be a single argument.\n\n**Proposed fix:** add two new options to `scripts/tracker add` and `scripts/tracker update` (and to any other subcommand that takes free-text input — `discuss`, `--note`, possibly `--title` for symmetry):\n\n1. **`--description-file <path>`** — read the description from a file. Content is treated as bytes/UTF-8; no shell interpretation.\n2. **`--description-stdin`** (or `--description -`) — read the description from stdin. Convenient for piping from heredocs or other process output without a temp file.\n\nThe two flags are mutually exclusive with each other and with `--description`. When more than one is given, exit non-zero with a clear error.\n\n**Prior art:** `git commit -F <file>` and `git commit --file=-`, `gh pr create --body-file`, `gh issue create --body-file`. Same problem, same standard solution.\n\n**Implementation surface:** `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/cli.py`. The argparse setup for `add`, `update`, `discuss` (and any other command with `--description`/`--note`) gets two more mutually-exclusive arguments; the dispatch site reads from file or stdin if either is set, otherwise falls back to the existing `--description` value. Pure additive change — no behavioral change for callers using the existing flag.\n\n**Tests:** assert that backticks, `$VAR`, single quotes, double quotes, newlines, and Unicode all round-trip through `--description-file` and `--description-stdin` unchanged. Assert that combining flags exits non-zero. Assert that `--description-file -` (or whatever the stdin sentinel is) works.\n\nNOT a governance file. Pure CLI improvement.\n\n**Source:** ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_04072026_1518.md is unrelated to this discovery; the bug was found while trackerizing that notebook. The conversation that surfaced it is in the live transcript; this item is the only persistent record."  # 935c
